CHUNKVAULT LARGE-SCALE STORAGE TEST DATA

ChunkVault is a chunk-based file storage system developed to demonstrate Operating System and Database Management System concepts. This test data is intended to evaluate file validation, adaptive chunking, buffered binary input and output, chunk identifiers, file identifiers, replica creation, and SHA-256 integrity verification.

A file storage system must preserve the contents of the original file throughout the entire storage process. When a file is divided into chunks, every chunk must contain the correct sequence of bytes. Each chunk should have a unique identifier and must remain associated with the correct original file. The chunk number is important because reconstruction requires the chunks to be combined in their original order.

Data integrity is an important requirement for reliable storage. ChunkVault calculates a SHA-256 checksum for each stored chunk. During integrity verification, the current checksum is compared against the checksum recorded when the chunk was originally stored. If both values match, the stored chunk is considered healthy. If the values differ, the chunk is considered corrupted. If the file cannot be found, the chunk is reported as missing.

Replication provides additional copies of stored data. ChunkVault creates replicas in separate logical storage locations. Each replica should be verified independently because one copy may become corrupted while another remains healthy. A recovery mechanism can use a verified healthy replica to restore a damaged or missing copy.

Large files provide useful test cases for evaluating buffered file operations. Reading and writing data in buffers avoids loading an entire large file into memory at once. The system can read a portion of the input file, write that portion to a chunk, and repeat the operation until all bytes have been processed. Correct handling of the final partial buffer is necessary to avoid losing or adding bytes.

Adaptive chunking selects a chunk size based on the input file size and the system's configured limits. The goal is to balance the number of chunks, metadata overhead, storage operations, and memory usage. A suitable chunk size depends on the design of the storage system and should be evaluated using actual performance measurements.

When multiple files are stored, each file must be identified independently. The storage system must not overwrite chunks belonging to another file. Metadata must preserve the relationship between the original file, its chunks, checksum values, and replica locations. Verification should report which file and which chunk has a problem.

Operating System scheduling can help organize multiple file-processing requests. First Come, First Served processes jobs in arrival order, while Shortest Job First selects the job with the shortest estimated processing time. These algorithms can be compared by measuring waiting time, turnaround time, and actual execution tim